[[RCD Mallorca|Mallorca]] qualified for the [[UEFA Cup Winners' Cup]] by finishing as runners-up in the Spanish [[Copa del Rey]]. They lost to [[FC Barcelona|Barcelona]] 5–4 in a [[Penalty shoot-out (association football)|penalty shootout]], but as Barcelona finished first in the [[1997–98 La Liga]], they qualified for the [[1998–99 UEFA Champions League]] and Mallorca were awarded their spot in the Cup Winners' Cup.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.rsssf.org/tabless/span98.html |title=Spain 1997/98 |date=26 January 2000 |access-date=3 May 2020 |first=Juan Pedro |website=[[RSSSF]] |last=Adrados }}</ref> Mallorca's opponents in the first round were Scottish team [[Heart of Midlothian F.C.|Heart of Midlothian]] (Hearts). They won the first leg, held at Hearts' home ground [[Tynecastle Park|Tynecastle]], 1–0.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/1998/sep/30/newsstory.sport3 |title=Celtic may find reasons to be careful |work=The Guardian |date=30 September 1998 |access-date=3 May 2020 |first=Patrick |last=Glenn }}</ref> The second leg was held at Mallorca's home ground, the [[Lluís Sitjar Stadium|Estadio Lluís Sitjar]]. Before the match, Hearts players protested that the goalposts at the same did not meet regulations. An inspection revealed there was a {{convert|10|cm|in|adj=on}} difference between one post and the other at both ends of the ground, this was due to the undulation of the pitch. Despite the issue, the match went ahead and finished 1–1. This meant Mallorca progressed to the second round 2–1 on aggregate.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/sport/football/184956.stm |title=Sport: Football Hearts get appeal date |website=BBC News |date=2 October 1998 |access-date=3 May 2020 }}</ref> [[K.R.C. Genk|Genk]] of Belgium were the opposition in the second round. The first leg held at the [[King Baudouin Stadium]] in Belgium finished 1–1. The second leg ended in a 0–0 draw, which meant Mallorca won the tie on the [[away goals rule]] to progress to the quarter-finals.<ref name=rsssf>{{cite web|url=https://www.rsssf.org/ec/ec199899.html |title=Cup Winners' Cup 1998–99 |date=4 June 2015 |access-date=3 May 2020 |first1=James M. |last=Ross |first2=Karel |website=[[RSSSF]] |last2=Stokkermans }}</ref>

Mallorca faced Croatian team [[NK Varaždin (1931–2015)|Varteks]] in the quarter-finals. The first leg, held at Varteks home ground, [[Stadion Varteks|Gradski Stadion]], finished 0–0.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://elpais.com/diario/1999/03/05/deportes/920588401_850215.html |title=El Mallorca negocia un buen resultado |work=El País |date=4 March 1999 |access-date=3 May 2020 |language=es |first=Patricio |last=Candia }}</ref> Three goals from [[Ariel Ibagaza]], [[Veljko Paunović]], and [[Dani García (footballer, born 1974)|Dani]], despite a late goal from Varteks defender, [[Andrija Balajić]], were enough to secure a 3–1 victory and their progression to the semi-finals.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://elpais.com/diario/1999/03/19/deportes/921798005_850215.html |title=El Mallorca logra una hazaña histórica |work=El País |date=18 March 1999 |access-date=3 May 2020 |language=es |first=Patricio |last=Candia }}</ref> Their opponents in the semi-finals were English team, [[Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]], who had won the competition the [[1997–98 UEFA Cup Winners' Cup|previous season]] and were looking to become the first team to retain the competition.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/these-football-times/2019/apr/11/cup-winners-cup-competition-retained |title=In praise of the Cup Winners' Cup, the competition that was never retained |work=The Guardian |date=11 April 2019 |access-date=3 May 2020 |first=Sam |last=Carney }}</ref> Mallorca went ahead in the first leg at Chelsea's home ground, [[Stamford Bridge (stadium)|Stamford Bridge]], when Dani went past Chelsea goalkeeper [[Ed de Goey]] to score. Chelsea equalised in the 50th minute when striker [[Tore André Flo]] scored.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/1999/apr/08/newsstory.sport10 |title=Chelsea 1–1 Mallorca |work=The Guardian |date=8 April 1999 |access-date=3 May 2020 }}</ref> Mallorca took an early lead in the second leg when [[Leonardo Biagini]] scored. Chelsea midfielder [[Roberto Di Matteo]] hit the crossbar 20 minutes from the end of the match and fellow midfielder [[Dennis Wise]] missed a header to equalise. They were unable to score and Mallorca won the match 1–0 to win 2–1 on aggregate and progress to the final in their first season in European competition.<ref name=biagini>{{cite news|url=https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football-chelsea-fall-to-biaginis-early-strike-1089074.html |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220501/https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football-chelsea-fall-to-biaginis-early-strike-1089074.html |archive-date=1 May 2022 |url-access=subscription |title=Football: Chelsea fall to Biagini's early strike |work=The Independent |date=23 April 1999 |access-date=3 May 2020 |first=Steve |last=Tongue }}{{cbignore}}</ref>

