Lazio qualified for the Cup Winners' Cup after they won the [[1997–98 Coppa Italia]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.rsssf.org/tablesi/ital98.html |title=Italy Championship 1997/98 |date=27 April 2017 |access-date=4 May 2020 |first=Maurizio |website=[[RSSSF]] |last=Mariani }}</ref> They beat [[A.C. Milan|Milan]] 3–2 on aggregate in the [[1998 Coppa Italia Final|final]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.rsssf.org/tablesi/italcup98.html |title=Coppa Italia 1997/98 |date=25 June 2005 |access-date=4 May 2020 |first=Roberto |website=[[RSSSF]] |last=Di Maggio }}</ref> Their opponents in the first round were Swiss team, [[FC Lausanne-Sport|Lausanne-Sport]]. The first leg finished in a 1–1 draw. The second leg also finished in a draw, this time the scores were 2–2. This meant that Lazio progressed to the second round due to the away goals rule. [[FK Partizan|Partizan]] of Yugoslavia were the opposition in the second round. The first leg ended in a 0–0 draw and Lazio won the second leg 3–2 to progress to the quarter-finals.<ref name=rsssf />

They faced Greek team [[Panionios F.C.|Panionios]], Lazio won the first leg 4–0 and were able to rest some of their first-team players in the second leg. Midfielders [[Pavel Nedvěd]] and [[Dejan Stanković]] both scored before fellow midfielder, [[Iván de la Peña]] secured a 3–0 victory to secure Lazio's progression to the semi-finals.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football-lazio-subdue-whelans-men-1081624.html |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220501/https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football-lazio-subdue-whelans-men-1081624.html |archive-date=1 May 2022 |url-access=subscription |title=Football: Lazio subdue Whelan's men |work=The Independent |date=19 March 1999 |access-date=4 May 2020 |first=John |last=Nisbet }}{{cbignore}}</ref> Their opponents were [[FC Lokomotiv Moscow|Lokomotiv Moscow]] of Russia. The Russian side went ahead in the first leg, at the [[RZD Arena|Lokomotiv Stadium]], when striker, [[Zaza Janashia]], scored in the 61st minute. Lazio equalised in the 77th minute when substitute [[Alen Bokšić]] scored. They almost won the match three minutes from full-time, but a shot by Stanković was saved by Lokomotiv goalkeeper [[Ruslan Nigmatullin]], the match finished 1–1.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football-boksic-boosts-lazio-1086102.html |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220501/https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football-boksic-boosts-lazio-1086102.html |archive-date=1 May 2022 |url-access=subscription |title=Football: Boksic boosts Lazio |work=The Independent |date=9 April 1999 |access-date=4 May 2020 }}{{cbignore}}</ref> The second leg was ended in a 0–0 draw, which meant Lazio progressed to the final on the away goals rule after the tie finished 1–1 on aggregate.<ref name=biagini />

==Match==
===Background===
[[File: Villa Park.jpg|left|thumb|250px|Villa Park hosted the final of the Cup Winners' Cup.]]
Mallorca were appearing in their first Cup Winners' Cup final in their first season in European competition. Lazio were also appearing in their first final in the competition, however, they had reached the final of the [[UEFA Europa League|UEFA Cup]] the [[1997–98 UEFA Cup|previous season]], a [[1998 UEFA Cup Final|match]] they lost 3–0 to fellow Italian team, [[Inter Milan|Internazionale]].<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.fourfourtwo.com/features/ronaldo-inter-milan-brazilian-italy-serie-a-europa-league-lazio-juventus-uefa-cup-final-injury-knee |title=When unplayable Ronaldo won the UEFA Cup with Inter Milan – but bad luck broke his shot at further glory |work=FourFourTwo |date=27 February 2020 |access-date=4 May 2020 }}</ref> The final was the last in the history of the competition. Founded in 1961, the Cup Winners' Cup was to be disbanded and incorporated into the UEFA Cup the following season.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://apnews.com/b20e1db8d8fb8a56c1e721ad49b435bd |title=Lazio Wins Cup Winners' Cup |agency=Associated Press|date=19 May 1999 |access-date=10 May 2020 }}</ref>

Mallorca's last match before the final was against [[Racing de Santander|Racing Santander]] in the [[1998–99 La Liga]], they lost 1–0.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.rsssf.org/tabless/span99.html |title=Spain 1998/99 |publisher=Rec. Sport. Soccer Statistics Foundation |date=26 January 2000 |access-date=5 May 2020 |first=Juan Pedro |last=Adrados }}</ref> Mallorca's record of 25 goals conceded in 33 games was the best in La Liga.<ref name=ellis>{{cite news|url=https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football-ellis-at-home-with-his-holiday-villa-1093887.html |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220501/https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football-ellis-at-home-with-his-holiday-villa-1093887.html |archive-date=1 May 2022 |url-access=subscription |title=Football: Ellis at home with his holiday Villa |work=The Independent |date=5 May 2020 |access-date=10 May 2020 |first=Steve |last=Tongue }}{{cbignore}}</ref> Lazio's final match before the final was a 1–1 draw with [[ACF Fiorentina|Fiorentina]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.rsssf.org/tablesi/ital99.html |title=Italy 1998/99 |date=9 July 2001 |access-date=5 May 2020 |first1=Davide |last1=Rota |first2=Sorin |last2=Arotaritei |first3=Misha |website=[[RSSSF]] |last3=Miladinovich }}</ref> The result meant they slipped behind into second with a game left to play in the [[1998–99 Serie A]] season.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.theblizzard.co.uk/article/journeys-end |title=Journey's End |work=The Blizzard |date=8 December 2019 |access-date=5 May 2020 |first=Thore |last=Haugstad |archive-date=1 September 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200901082824/https://www.theblizzard.co.uk/article/journeys-end |url-status=dead }}</ref> The Cup Winners' Cup final was held at the home of [[Aston Villa F.C.|Aston Villa]], [[Villa Park]] in Birmingham. The chairman of Aston Villa, [[Doug Ellis]], considered Lazio to be the favourites: "Lazio are favourites if you listen to the bookmakers, but Mallorca have some very, very good players."<ref name=ellis />

Lazio manager, [[Sven-Göran Eriksson]], was adamant his side were eager to win the Cup Winners' Cup despite the possibility of winning Serie A the weekend after the final: "This is not a secondary objective. We must find the grit and determination, the courage and the energy, to win the cup." The difference in the two teams was emphasised before the final. Lazio had spent £73 million on new players the previous summer, while Mallorca were considered "an unfashionable island side" by ''[[The Guardian]]''. This did not bother Mallorca striker Dani, who was adamant they could compete with Lazio: "We are conscious of the huge difference between the squads{{nbsp}}... but we make up for that with concentration and teamwork to get results."<ref name=guardianpreview>{{cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/1999/may/19/newsstory.sport3 |title=Lazio out to raise their stock |work=The Guardian |date=19 May 1999 |access-date=1 May 2020 |first=Russell |last=Thomas }}</ref>

The choice of Austrian, [[Günter Benkö]], as referee concerned the Italian media. ''[[Corriere dello Sport]]'' called him a "scandalous referee." They referred to him as "a sworn enemy of Italian squads," after he had [[Fouls and misconduct (association football)#Red card (dismissal)|sent off]] two Italian players in a UEFA under-21 international in 1996.<ref name=guardianpreview />

===Summary===
[[File:Christian Vieri (cropped).jpg|upright|thumb|[[Christian Vieri]] scored the opening goal and was named man of the match.]]
Both teams lined up in a [[Formation (association football)#4–4–2|4-4-2 formation]] with four defenders, four midfielders and two strikers.<ref name=shaw>{{cite news|url=https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football-nedved-wins-final-prize-for-lazio-1094779.html |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220501/https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football-nedved-wins-final-prize-for-lazio-1094779.html |archive-date=1 May 2022 |url-access=subscription |url-status=live |title=Football: Nedved wins final prize for Lazio |work=The Independent |date=20 May 1999 |access-date=9 May 2020 |first=Phil |last=Shaw }}{{cbignore}}</ref> The opening minutes of the match were "breathtakingly open", according to ''The Guardian.'' A Vieri shot from {{convert|25|yd|m}} was saved by Mallorca goalkeeper, [[Carlos Roa]].<ref name=guardian /> The first goal was scored in the seventh minute when striker [[Christian Vieri]] headed a pass from [[Giuseppe Favalli]] past Roa.<ref name=shaw /> Four minutes later, Mallorca had equalised. [[Miquel Soler]] passed to [[Jovan Stanković]] who [[Cross (association football)|crossed]] from the right-hand side of the pitch which was converted past Lazio goalkeeper, [[Luca Marchegiani]], by Dani.<ref name=guardian /> Vieri was injured when Roa's hand caught him in the head.<ref name=guardian /> He was carried off the field midway through the first half and his head bandaged before he returned.<ref name=shaw /> A long-range shot from Vieri was saved by Roa, while a Mallorca [[Free kick (association football)|free-kick]] taken by Jovan Stanković passed through the Lazio penalty area without anyone touching the ball. A [[bicycle kick]] by Lazio striker [[Marcelo Salas]], which Roa saved, was the last chance before the first half came to an end with the scores at 1–1.<ref name=shaw />

According to Phil Shaw, writing in ''[[The Independent]]'', "the open play which had characterised the start of the contest was markedly absent as the second period unfolded."<ref name=shaw /> Mallorca started the better of the two teams with [[Lauren (Cameroonian footballer)|Lauren]] causing problems down the right-hand side of the pitch.<ref name=irish>{{cite news|url=https://www.irishtimes.com/sport/lazio-the-last-of-the-cup-winners-1.186896? |title=Lazio the last of the cup winners |work=The Irish Times |date=20 May 1999 |access-date=1 May 2020 }}</ref> Dejan Stanković was caught in the face by Mallorca defender, [[Gustavo Siviero]] as he made an overhead clearance. Siviero was shown a [[Penalty card#Yellow card|yellow card]] and Dejan Stanković was replaced by [[Sérgio Conceição]] following the incident.<ref name=guardian /> Lazio had the best chance of the second half in the 60th minute when a free-kick by defender [[Siniša Mihajlović]], from {{convert|32|yd|m}} went just wide of the Mallorca goal. Minutes later, [[Roberto Mancini]] had a chance to score, but his shot hit the crossbar and he was deemed to be offside.<ref name=irish /> A shot by Lauren was saved by Lazio goalkeeper, Marchegiani, minutes later.<ref name=shaw /> Mallorca had another chance to score as Jovan Stanković made his way into the Lazio penalty area. His shot went over the head of Marchegiani, but [[Alessandro Nesta]] headed the ball away from the Lazio goal.<ref name=irish /> With nine minutes left to play, Lazio scored. Nedvěd volleyed a clearance past Roa in the Mallorca from {{convert|20|yd|m}}.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/sport/blog/2017/dec/08/the-joy-of-six-cup-winners-cup-memories |title=The Joy of Six: Cup Winners' Cup memories |work=The Guardian |date=8 December 2017 |access-date=10 May 2020 |first1=Michael |last1=Butler |first2=Daniel |last2=Harris |first3=Jacob |last3=Steinberg }}</ref> Nedvěd was substituted after the goal and with no further goals scored, Lazio won the match 2–1 to win the last ever Cup Winners' Cup.<ref name=irish />

